#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n=\n\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} \\! \\int_{0}^2 \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\textrm{d}r \\: \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084 :topic/difficulty 0.7620038433200931 :topic/id #uuid "d952f888-5e27-5f27-a3ff-b83b56acd32a" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275}] :topic/math-academy-id 2030 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016}] :topic/title "Double Integrals in Plane Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070 :topic/difficulty 1.061673902190143 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428635 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacade