{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da61f6d3-ed49-54d2-b863-5a31a5f2ff0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537665} :answer-field/id #uuid "9608a74f-b086-5f31-b192-ae95683f2fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174124}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e77d34a7-9f25-5206-895c-5f4fa0d1f689" :question/math-academy-id "q-125716" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/d8/d831c7fb805d16ec7a2dc2462813ea79b78d888ed2121330ed5d823b590d3c4f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+(x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r-{r}^{3}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfeea07-4dc9-5945-8f32-4ba04d4c39db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0c1485-b37d-59e9-8042-95dc6071d26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416094} {:answer/id #uuid "a7529453-a85e-5454-aaea-6763faa38e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416096} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f2f158-1952-54d5-b338-cc98999f91b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8487d6d-ade8-5e6b-aa72-6dcb7d480679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdd86cce-9e5b-5a18-9af7-54a7f6efd03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41469" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e93c4c-784f-5084-ab8a-83b41a9c3b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259887} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd94e5-4741-54cd-91f5-397f8394cacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260121} {:answer/id #uuid "26253928-a96d-5ca2-ada8-659d943f2a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260209} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} {:answer/id #uuid "d06bbafa-a9ef-5c6e-ae57-d690593cedb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b35c4ee-3674-511e-b02e-606a2c68404b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262230}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d73aed01-512e-5f02-83f5-67f605d164e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126526" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(x+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e6/e69bc348e36b05955d1eaa9eda4516afe137056ead176a46a4fc11bb887ed166.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b383de-3cfc-58c9-9df3-f58fbe8d7752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259884} {:answer/id #uuid "27f0dae3-025f-5ca9-b20c-451a7f1885e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260225} {:answer/id #uuid "5a872dfd-1e43-501e-90a1-8c43483bc0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260649} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdd8636-0e7b-56ac-90ac-1af7ffaabbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260958} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e7086f-0275-5f12-b2e7-d454e0bef91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a872dfd-1e43-501e-90a1-8c43483bc0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260649} :answer-field/id #uuid "d584d815-5b73-56a5-8b00-752a8a746831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ee4972b-d705-5cec-8dc7-f572de324a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-126542" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/5f/5f7d53fb7692663cca39f718491b4dccb6244031e2b7c75f766b7a8340696d64.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 4,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e498e93-5b24-5605-b258-5e7001367f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260845} {:answer/id #uuid "7d508f6f-4b62-5ad5-b049-acd045b555fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260965} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c0d43-3607-57f6-bbfe-2ed08e93ae7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261621} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bf830d-1446-55f2-9cff-18bc217fc8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261631} {:answer/id #uuid "e2636c40-db77-5979-a0a0-94fab8a3c54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c62c0d43-3607-57f6-bbfe-2ed08e93ae7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261621} :answer-field/id #uuid "59816d41-8481-5201-be15-dbee5b961603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262277}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db4bf9cd-fbd1-5e73-9e78-d4f96b20828d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126567" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.222em}\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3e/3e378b8f7731dbdbc44c232e9b8703067f39a5b3f836934afcbacb57d47972b2.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 5,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3{r}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21e75e6-c690-5426-9022-4e65c4cd597f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405038} {:answer/id #uuid "858df234-13ff-5820-8cad-fd7b874bd17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405039} {:answer/id #uuid "132a6cae-f594-52cb-8c0a-5014dac8b218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} {:answer/id #uuid "289d6efd-e337-519a-9f4f-3e9ef2eb9804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041} {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f53d24-ffcb-51fb-8ef8-920f533f7ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" :question/math-academy-id "q-126539" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 6,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017f8f24-8529-55cc-a1a8-41dc999d12f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0e048-b5e5-51cc-8ee4-8a54d22a60bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405046} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65de8-c1f0-596d-875a-4aa5f454bb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047} {:answer/id #uuid "0592c862-0b50-54dc-acf8-8691c79146ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405048} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+(x+2y)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ff21cb-9e33-53f3-b627-a88a79716c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416073} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf3164-1e19-59b8-a518-c866ee98eb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416079} {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} {:answer/id #uuid "989f1950-08af-5b66-a148-ca15e1457577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416091} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b8980a-616f-5aa2-877a-5c0ee10376d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} :answer-field/id #uuid "634f078a-6dcb-548c-8f80-dad54be93eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d616e0c6-f6b2-5a83-83cf-32e007120166" :question/math-academy-id "q-126569" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y(x-1),\\hspace{0.222em}x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}x+x(y-1)\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/b9/b95320928a5e7482f9ba2b8bb1d1499950710545b4c7273b7f70dfe5ab8c84b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((y-1)-(x-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f21bd52-4aa7-5ea8-a034-d3560d1d5b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458686} {:answer/id #uuid "648aec14-e325-59f4-a6ad-4fe7fed0f4d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458801} {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} {:answer/id #uuid "76be6580-ecd9-54fd-be34-2e6d6eb3bf26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458837} {:answer/id #uuid "867e3063-71a8-5b3f-ba08-0e4184738773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c53cfdb-2fdd-546b-ab1b-dc3144c77b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458818} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb86058-0e7f-5528-b79d-a77b82e246b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459138}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fe30a0-5320-52ad-9ed5-45000abef204" :question/math-academy-id "q-126532" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/04/0470588a034222b183694b087d417e8ead0d2fd72357e796373794d00523708f.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x+y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(x+y)\\,dx-{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2xy-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5d1032-d8df-5131-9315-d27ce3d12daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537247} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8a5c91-67b1-5ae9-83b6-a14d25c55b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537296} {:answer/id #uuid "46824a5c-5d89-5cc0-92ba-1b68ec6d9595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537373} {:answer/id #uuid "9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537525} {:answer/id #uuid "eb337268-12ae-51d7-ad27-55abc1397eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e873ca7-af38-5f44-9341-1fb3e1ea8712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537525} :answer-field/id #uuid "600ccac0-481d-5135-af9b-c1f28719ac01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37e413b5-1ec6-5915-aa78-dfcf790b6b22" :question/math-academy-id "q-126519" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{x}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{3}\\,dx-{x}^{3}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}(-3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta -3{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}-3{r}^{3}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3{r}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} {:answer/id #uuid "307eac12-8ffa-5b27-b7eb-9fa2f8ffbd12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537332} {:answer/id #uuid "4697b16a-b954-5ce9-9fdc-2eb3d67c270a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537374} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79f07c-c8a1-549a-81cf-2a1164ba3d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537386} {:answer/id #uuid "656fd776-3d31-5e32-af9a-81ea6f3a0abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e31ec7e-c4a0-5af1-a007-a5f374623cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537294} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf819579-d6fd-53c1-af84-bcfb66bf0607" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537858}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af0d631e-6f1f-57fd-bd84-ccd6cde384ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-126546" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\hspace{-0.167em}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\enclose{box}{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}x},\\hspace{0.222em}x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/0c/0cb340ef33d4cc38081e3796b4c8e076f1499760c0a3ffeecb8123a41fefeec8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y+{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\,dx+x{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1edb3c-53e2-59fb-8b25-bd49ec07d7ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (3 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563818} {:answer/id #uuid "179e0ce9-20d2-5083-94bb-403ba2113db7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563820} {:answer/id #uuid "367d7cc1-3324-54fc-9fb9-97d117004d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{2} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563830} {:answer/id #uuid "802b78f8-6827-5cd8-9458-b108ba1aa055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563842} {:answer/id #uuid "e66f5319-c723-5e2c-94aa-56c9c8ccafda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e66f5319-c723-5e2c-94aa-56c9c8ccafda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563856} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba192ad9-824f-5b2a-9948-ce270cf9499b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21cd5573-a0e4-5bef-86ec-1c6e167cab52" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/47/47ee81cb3603c75c8a83dca2df1153733902576892fa6eed3df4bccd329655ee.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-18{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-18{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}-18{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ec3af8-1efb-5bac-9653-b4bbeb227b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563825} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef44ce3-1fed-5c9e-bfcd-9fa6a3cf53c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 6 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563836} {:answer/id #uuid "618036f5-1521-5d86-bd10-2233ad57f06b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563838} {:answer/id #uuid "90d63d22-f3b5-5dc8-bace-ac4440bf2edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563843} {:answer/id #uuid "9dfc6627-6984-5941-a167-46191993853d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ec3af8-1efb-5bac-9653-b4bbeb227b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563825} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dbf35a0-0cac-5fb0-af95-6fe98a827932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ec57f20-b633-5781-9a09-b50d332a0f91" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-6{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -9{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563837 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a264f90-1842-5756-9963-c3e6d9be87ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (3 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563832} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1488b1-b1e1-5fd9-87fb-515370820925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563833} {:answer/id #uuid "9c43a0f5-ae70-5b97-94e4-44c82b646d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563845} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c4fdfd-aa50-5fc2-9e7b-5dce4c57d69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563853} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e3f26c-fa8e-53df-9b90-6d6c1bed7662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c43a0f5-ae70-5b97-94e4-44c82b646d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563845} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e08bdb-12c4-5eb6-9fe4-23f4e1ced610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cc10faf-7aa0-5392-84df-19592fc46175" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b74ee14-79d5-5359-a0d9-6f49e75fed34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563827} {:answer/id #uuid "36bc7de8-7904-5380-a4ad-90310a70debf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563831} {:answer/id #uuid "61d95760-e2fb-5368-aeae-0aafe98a48c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563839} {:answer/id #uuid "9f30cc5a-a2b3-5654-bec7-9886e53e4674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563848} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d82cf7-12ef-5422-a34d-58ca680840ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (7 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f30cc5a-a2b3-5654-bec7-9886e53e4674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (\\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563848} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6939241-4a4f-5cfa-bde1-319c8cc79271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63ff3918-bbda-5dba-a275-8bd09f72999d" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/06/06ecd3e3f2b2538556ffb2ffece12564b621f997ef8bf6de0e3297c7b86b393b.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=12{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-9{x}^{2}-12{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-12\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-9{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -12{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1add22ad-9d01-5471-b785-9f130d84b86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563822} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0bcb58-3bdc-5cef-adc1-fd56c920b832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (7 \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563823} {:answer/id #uuid "333ff705-2b09-5376-86d1-0978a1ff962e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 3 r^{3} \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563829} {:answer/id #uuid "c96602c9-23ac-55d2-adc5-91df2cf085a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{2} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563852} {:answer/id #uuid "f00bab6e-d734-5d82-af2a-a6a62be6bf74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 r^{3} \\sin^{2}{(\\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1add22ad-9d01-5471-b785-9f130d84b86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 r^{3} (5 \\cos^{2}{(\\theta )} - 6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563822} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b1678d-9a6c-5b52-8417-f48a18624db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66b94c75-2352-5ce1-9d02-093a1a6011c2" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/ee/ee7096a5ce1f420ac962f93b0efb47a00cdc2c9d9ec69f66b6340d02dd106b77.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=18{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,drd\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-18{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-18\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -18{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05641351-2500-5b28-8370-c1b90886418c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563817} {:answer/id #uuid "0f9e4b41-f88b-5423-a2a5-30a98f05dfc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563819} {:answer/id #uuid "2629946e-bdd6-534f-937c-a51e26936186" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} \\cos{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563826} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9f3cf2-1e76-5612-b0e4-9e6548b89957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r \\cos{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563835} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c7afcd-45bb-58b8-a0f7-d3c30eb428c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05641351-2500-5b28-8370-c1b90886418c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 r^{2} (\\sin{(\\theta )} - \\cos{(\\theta )})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5a9e1a-563c-5f72-836e-afd8b876a1c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "969a550c-c4ff-5d52-a5c3-2f0c19e8dcb1" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨6y(x-1),\\,6x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/fb/fbf107e5d55e2dd51307b821d9d2f5134810317c69706a57f8d9aac528f78e93.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=6y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=6x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6(x-1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((6(y-1))-(6(x-1)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(6(y-x))\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(6(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c53d44-953b-562a-b3b2-38f6c6051bfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563861} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0b2dd8-7329-5565-a82f-fbdf918e0d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563863} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a670e0-35ba-52fa-b476-0a05dc1679bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 r^{3} \\sin{(2 \\theta )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563864} {:answer/id #uuid "ece15ad0-2be9-57bd-af7c-a5fd555eba51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563865} {:answer/id #uuid "fa596e02-58e8-5f26-9fc1-448d9b654999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b0b2dd8-7329-5565-a82f-fbdf918e0d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563863} :answer-field/id #uuid "46c59553-15cf-5320-87d6-38643dd06fe9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02fd691d-ae6a-5f3c-bb90-c16fde492236" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ,\n$$\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}]}
